# Attenuation design

How a Fearless program gets less than the full `System`, and how a main runs another main of the same program in a new process with the same limits or more.

### Fearless facts

`System` is the root object capability: every observation of the world goes through a method of `System` or of a capability `System` gives (`CapTry`, `Output`, `InputCursor`, `AssetReadCapability`, `DownloadCapability`, `CapDataRead`, `CapDataReadWrite`, `CapManager`, `CapProcess`, `LogReader`, `Gui`).
`System` and every capability it gives are not sealed: Fearless code can implement them.
A main is `Main: CaptureFree { .main(sys: mut System): Void }`; the runtime gives it the one real `System` of the process.
Observable Determinism: a method with no mut parameters, receiver included, returns structurally equivalent results for structurally equivalent inputs; non termination and deterministic exceptions are results.
A non deterministic exception is caught only by a `CapTry`, and a `CapTry` comes only from a `System`.
A type implementing `CaptureFree` captures nothing: its instance is a singleton, and its type name denotes that singleton. A top level type captures nothing by construction; a type declared inside a method captures nothing only when it implements `CaptureFree`.
The Fearless manager runs each main in a JVM of its own, started with the folder of the project and the data projects of its links (ControllersDesign, Jobs and Data projects of a running main).
`Info` is the one value with a text form that parses back: `Info.str` is JSON text, `Infos.fromStr` reads it.

### Terms

From the object capability literature, used here with one meaning each:
- 'object capability': an object that can do a privileged thing; holding a reference to it is the permission to do that thing. `System` and every capability it gives are object capabilities.
- 'authority': what a program can do to the world. A capability carries authority; a `mut System` carries all of it.
- 'attenuation': deriving from a capability a capability with less authority. An 'attenuator' of a capability type `C` is a function from a `mut C` to a `mut C` with less authority. Not: limiter, watchdog, restriction, filter.
- 'revoke': to take the authority away from a capability already given. A capability 'revoked' is one with no authority: every method of it throws. 'start': to give a capability its authority.
- 'capture free': the Fearless `CaptureFree` promise. An attenuator is capture free, so it is named by its type.
- 'reconstruction': building in a new process the attenuated `System` of another process from the names of its attenuators, instead of transferring any object.

## Current design

Everything below is in `base/attenuation.fear`, in the runtime `rt/_CapProcess$18g$0.java`, and in the `Main.java` the backend writes for each package.

### Attenuators

```
Attenuator[C]: CaptureFree, F[mut C, mut C]{}
SystemAttenuator: Attenuator[System]{}
```
An attenuator of `System` receives a `mut System` and gives a `mut System` whose methods reach the world only through the received one.
It is capture free, so its type name identifies it everywhere the program runs.
An attenuator that keeps a capability writes the method as a delegation, `.out -> u.out`; one that revokes a capability throws from the method that gives it; one that attenuates a capability applies an attenuator of that capability, `.gui -> NoFullScreen#(u.gui)` with `NoFullScreen: Attenuator[Gui]`.
The error of a revoked capability is non deterministic: for the attenuated program it is the environment refusing, and only a `CapTry` catches it.
```
NoDownload: SystemAttenuator { u -> {'s
  .try -> u.try; .out -> u.out; .inputCursor -> u.inputCursor; .assetRead -> u.assetRead;
  .download -> Error.nonDeterministic "NoDownload: System.download is revoked.";
  .dataRead -> u.dataRead; .dataReadWrite -> u.dataReadWrite; .manager -> u.manager; .process -> u.process;
  .readLog l -> u.readLog l; .gui -> u.gui;
  .iso -> NoDownload#(u.iso.close); .close -> s;
  }}
OnlyOutput: SystemAttenuator { u -> mut ErrorSystem{'s .try -> u.try; .out -> u.out; .iso -> OnlyOutput#(u.iso.close); .close -> s } }
```
`NoDownload` revokes one capability and delegates the others; `OnlyOutput` keeps two and revokes the others by extending `ErrorSystem`, overriding `.iso` and `.close` too, since the `.iso` of `ErrorSystem` is an `ErrorSystem`.
Attenuators compose by application: `NoManager#(NoDownload#(sys))` gives a `System` with neither capability, whatever the order; an outer attenuator reaches only what the inner one gives, so no attenuator recovers authority.

### ErrorSystem and StartRevokeSystem

```
ErrorSystem: System { ...every method throws...; .iso -> ErrorSystem; .close -> this; }
StartRevokeSystem: System {
  mut .start(sys: mut System): Void;
  mut .revoke: Void;
  }
StartRevokeSystems: { #: mut StartRevokeSystem -> ... }
```
`ErrorSystem` is a `System` with no authority: every method throws the non deterministic error `System.<method> is revoked.`, `.iso` gives another `ErrorSystem` and `.close` gives itself.
It is a type, not a value, so a test uses `ErrorSystem` as the `System` of a main that must not touch the world, and an attenuator that keeps a few capabilities extends it (`OnlyOutput` above).

`StartRevokeSystems#` gives a `System` in one of three states, in this order and with no way back: not started, started, revoked.
Not started: every method throws the non deterministic error `System.<method> is revoked: .start was not called yet.`.
Started, by `.start(sys)`: every method delegates to `sys`.
Revoked, by `.revoke`: every method throws the non deterministic error `System.<method> is revoked: .revoke was called.`.
`.start` on a started or revoked `StartRevokeSystem`, and `.revoke` on one not started or already revoked, are deterministic errors: the holder of a `StartRevokeSystem` knows its state, since only its own calls change it.
`StartRevokeSystems#` is written in Fearless: a `Var[mut Opt[mut System]]` and a `Var[Bool]` telling whether `.start` ran.
`.iso` delegates as every other method: on a started `StartRevokeSystem` it gives the iso image of the `System` passed to `.start`.
Revocation reaches the calls on the `StartRevokeSystem` itself. What its methods gave before `.revoke` (an `Output`, a `CapTry`, the iso image) belongs to the `System` passed to `.start` and stays usable. The open question 'Depth of revocation' below discusses reaching those too.

### Deterministic reconstruction

The runtime applies attenuators as follows, in every process it starts for a main:
```
Block#
  .let s= {StartRevokeSystems#}
  .let attenuated= {An#(...(A1#(s)))}
  .do{ s.start(realSystem) }
  .return{ main.main(attenuated) }
```
`A1`...`An` are the attenuators of the process, in order; `A1` is applied first, so it is the closest to the real `System`, and `An` is what the main sees.
While the attenuators run, `s` is not started: every call on it throws a non deterministic error, and nothing in the process holds a `CapTry` yet, since every `System` reachable by the attenuators is `s` or something built from it, and `s.try` throws. So an attenuator either does not call `s` while it runs, or ends the process. Attenuators are capture free, so `s` is their only mut input. By Observable Determinism, the `System` the attenuators build is structurally equivalent in every process applying the same names in the same order: the construction is a deterministic computation, with the one mut input muted.
The real `System` enters only after the construction, through `.start`: no Fearless code of the process ever holds the real `System` itself.
An attenuator calls `u.iso` only from its own `.iso`, which runs after `.start`: `.iso` of a not started `StartRevokeSystem` throws as every other method.
Consequences:
- a process is fully described by the program, a main name and a list of attenuator names: no object crosses a process boundary;
- the Fearless manager applies attenuators by name without running anything else of the program;
- a main starting another main passes only names, and the new process rebuilds the same attenuation, then adds its own.

### Processes

```
System: ... { mut .process: mut CapProcess; }
CapProcess: ToIso[CapProcess], WidenTo[CapProcess]{
  mut .run(main: Main, attenuators: List[SystemAttenuator]): Void;
  }
```
`sys.process.run(SecondMain, List#(OtherAttenuator))` starts a new process of this program running `SecondMain`, waits for its end, and ends as `SecondMain.main` ended: it returns `Void` when `SecondMain` completes, it throws the deterministic error `SecondMain` threw, it throws the non deterministic error `SecondMain` threw. Running a main in a new process reads as calling it: `sys.process.run(SecondMain, as)` behaves as `SecondMain.main(An#(...A1#(sys)))`, with `as` the attenuators `A1`...`An`, except that the call runs in a process of its own, so the end of the call releases everything the call held (see below). A `Try#` around the call catches what `SecondMain` threw deterministically, a `CapTry` catches both kinds, as for a direct call.
The new process applies the attenuators of this process, in their order, then `attenuators` in their order: the authority of the new process is the authority of this one or less, and it can not be more. An empty list adds no attenuator.
The new process starts as this one started: same first input file, same data projects, same manager, same desk identity, same working folder; it prints to the output of this process. Each process has windows of its own: a file dropped on a window of the new process arrives on the `InputCursor` of the new process, as a file dropped on a window of this process arrives on this one. Files that arrived on this process are not input files of the new process.
Every main is capture free, so every main has a name: `Main` implements `CaptureFree`, and a main declared inside a method captures nothing. `.run` accepts every main of the program, the mains of every package included, not only the mains of the highest ranking package.
A process that can not start, or that ends before its main ends (a JVM error, a kill), is a non deterministic error of `.run`.
The process boundary is where revocation is deep: ending a process revokes everything it holds, also what it obtained through a `StartRevokeSystem` before `.revoke`.
The end of a process also ends every process it started: a new process holds the parent lifeline of its parent, as every process the manager starts does.

Runtime mechanics (an implementation decides these, the design only needs them to exist):
- the attenuators of a process are the system property `fearlessAttenuators`, the qualified type names separated by commas; no property means no attenuator;
- the `Main.java` of each package lists its mains and its attenuators, each name next to its instance, so the runtime goes from a name to the instance (in the new process) and from an instance to its name (in the calling process); an anonymous capture free literal `{u -> ...}` of type `SystemAttenuator` is an attenuator with the name the backend gives to anonymous literals;
- the new JVM is the `java` of `java.home` with the input arguments of this JVM, the class path of this JVM, the `Main` class of the package of the main and the name of the main; the properties `fearlessAttenuators`, `fearlessProcess.port` and `fearless.endMarker` are not inherited;
- the result travels on a loopback port the calling process listens on: `ok`, or `det` or `nondet` followed by the JSON text of the `Info`; the calling process reads it after the new process ended and rethrows the `Info` with the same kind;
- a process with a port does not print its uncaught `Info`: the `Info` reaches the calling process, which prints it as its own uncaught error if nothing catches it, so an `Info` is printed once, by the process it escapes from; the stack frames of the new process do not travel, the `Info` does;
- the standard output of this process is flushed before the new process starts, so the two outputs do not interleave out of order.

### Manager

Nothing below is implemented yet.

The compiler records the attenuators of a project where it records its mains: `.fearless_out/<pkg>.manifest.info` per compiled package, assembled into `.fearless_out/manifest.info` after every compile (today these files are `<pkg>.mains.info` and `mains.info`, and hold the mains only). The manifest is an Info object with the mains of every package, as `mains.info` lists them today, and the attenuators of every package, each mapped to its declaring file:
```
{
  "mains": { "hello.Hello1": [...], ... },
  "attenuators": { "hello.NoDownload": "_hello/attenuators.fear", "hello.NoGui": "_hello/attenuators.fear" }
}
```
The attenuators of a code project are the static list of the `SystemAttenuator` types it declares; they are known exactly when its mains are known.

`projects.info` gets, for a code project, `attenuators`: for each main, the ordered list of the attenuators the manager applies to it; a main absent from `attenuators` gets none:
```
"hello_world": {
  "path": ...,
  "kind": "code",
  "mains": ["hello.Hello1"],
  "attenuators": {"hello.Hello1": ["hello.NoDownload", "hello.NoGui"]},
  ...
```
A `Run` starts each main with `-DfearlessAttenuators=` and its list, so the main sees what the attenuators give.
A new verb, `Attenuate <name> <main> <attenuator>...`, sets the list of attenuators of `main` to the attenuators given, in that order; with no attenuator the list is empty. `CapManager` gets `mut .attenuate(name: Str, main: Str, attenuators: List[Str]): mut Action[Void]`.
Ill formed: an `Attenuate` naming a main that is not a main of the project, or an attenuator that is not an attenuator of the project (the message lists them), or the same attenuator twice; a `Run` of a main whose entry in `projects.info` names an attenuator that is not an attenuator of the project any more (the message names it: the list is edited, or the cache is out of date).
The project area gets, for a code project with attenuators, an Attenuators collapsible area after the Output: one block per main, listing in order the attenuators applied to it, each with a Remove button and Up and Down buttons, and a drop down of the attenuators of the project with an Add button. Every change is an `Attenuate` of that main.
The docs of the project (Open docs) show the attenuators as they show the mains, with their doc comments: a doc comment of an attenuator says what it revokes.

### Properties

- No recovery: an attenuator reaches only what it receives; a new process reaches only what its attenuators give; the real `System` is held by the runtime alone. The authority of a process is monotone along the tree of processes.
- Deterministic reconstruction: the attenuated `System` of a new process is structurally equivalent to the one its parent would build with the same names, by Observable Determinism of the construction (see Deterministic reconstruction).
- Fail loud: an attenuator calling the received `System` while it runs ends the process with a non deterministic error, in every process, at the first run.
- An attenuator is static: the policy is in the names, chosen in `projects.info` or in the code of the calling main, never in a value computed at run time. This is what makes a list of names a faithful description of the authority.
- Process call is method call: `.run` ends as the main it runs ends, with the same `Void`, the same deterministic error or the same non deterministic error.

### Limits

- State of an attenuator is per process. An attenuator counting calls (a download quota) starts from zero in every new process, so a main can get a fresh quota by starting a child. A quota attenuator that must hold across processes revokes `.process`, or counts the processes it starts and shares the quota among them.
- Revocation through a `StartRevokeSystem` reaches the calls on that `StartRevokeSystem`; the deep revocation is ending the process.
- An attenuator of `Gui` wraps every `mut Gui` the builder methods return, and the `Frame` and widgets `.run` gives: it is as long as the `Gui` API. The getters of `Gui` are mut methods (`mut .screenWidth`), so a `Gui` attenuator answers them as it likes.
- Each `.run` starts a JVM: tenths of a second, not microseconds. A process is the unit of attenuation, not a function call.
- The attenuated program sees the stack frames of the attenuator in the errors the attenuator throws, when a `CapTry` catches them.
- Two mains of the same project started with the same desk identity: the desk shows the windows of the new process under the identity of the main the manager started. The manager could pass one identity per main of the project.

### Open questions

- Depth of revocation. With `.revoke` reaching only the calls on the `StartRevokeSystem`, a program that gave away `s.out` keeps printing after `s.revoke`. The deeper option: every method of a started `StartRevokeSystem` wraps what it gives in a literal of the same capability type that checks the state at every call, one level deep (an `Output` wrapper, a `CapTry` wrapper, ...), so `.revoke` reaches every capability obtained through `s`. The cost is one wrapper type per capability type, each as long as that type; `Gui` needs the `Frame` and widgets too, or stops at `.run`. The thorny part is `.iso`: an `iso System` is, by its type, disconnected from every mut of the program, and the state of `s` is a mut `Var`; a pure Fearless wrapper of `s.iso` can not share the state of `s`, so what `.iso` gives escapes revocation, and a `CapTry` body gets its `System` from `.iso` (`sys.try#(sys.iso.close, {s' -> ...})`): with deep wrappers and a pure Fearless `.iso`, revocation reaches nothing inside a `CapTry` body. A `.iso` that shares the state of `s` needs the runtime (`_Magic!`); it is sound from the point of view of the holder of the image, since every observation through a `System` is non deterministic and a revocation reads as the world refusing. The choice: (a) revocation at the `StartRevokeSystem` only, pure Fearless, as implemented; (b) deep wrappers with `.iso` escaping, pure Fearless, with the `CapTry` hole; (c) deep wrappers with a magic `.iso` sharing the state. The runtime needs only `.start`, which every option gives the same way.
- Names. `Attenuator`, `SystemAttenuator`, `ErrorSystem`, `StartRevokeSystem`, `CapProcess`.
- Where attenuators are applied in the portable Fearless: the portable runs every main with no attenuator; a project could carry a default list in a file the portable reads, or the portable stays plain.
- The `Output` marks of a job: the manager marks where each main starts and ends; the output of a new process is inside the output of its parent main, with no mark of its own.

### Second step: a process that runs beside its parent

```
CapProcess: ... {
  mut .start(main: Main, attenuators: List[SystemAttenuator]): mut RunningMain;
  }
RunningMain: ToIso[RunningMain], WidenTo[RunningMain]{
  mut .await: Void;
  mut .awaitUpTo(millis: Nat): Bool;
  mut .terminate: Void;
  }
```
`.start` starts the process and returns at once. `.await` ends as `.run` ends. `.awaitUpTo` waits up to the time given and tells whether the process ended; a process that ended is then awaited with `.await`, which ends at once. `.terminate` ends the process and every process it started; `.await` on a terminated process is a non deterministic error. A method that can fail only because of the environment (`.start`, `.terminate`) throws a non deterministic error, as `.run` does.
Timeouts become deterministic from the point of view of the program: `start`, `awaitUpTo`, `terminate` is a computation whose result is the main completing or the main cut; the cut is clean because the process boundary is where revocation is deep.

### Third step: values across processes

An `Info` is the one value with a text form that parses back, so it is the first value to cross:
```
InfoMain: CaptureFree { .main(sys: mut System, arg: Info): Info }
CapProcess: ... {
  mut .run(main: InfoMain, arg: Info, attenuators: List[SystemAttenuator]): Info;
  }
```
The argument travels to the new process with the names, the result travels back with the `ok` line. Any immutable value with `ToInfo` and a parser from `Info` crosses this way; a general `imm` transfer needs a serialization protocol for every immutable type, which Fearless does not have yet, and which `DataType.info` is a start of.

### Related work and the paper

Object capabilities: an object capability is an object that can do a privileged thing, and the reference to it is the permission (Miller, Robust Composition, 2006; Miller, Yee, Shapiro, Capability Myths Demolished, 2003). Attenuation, the caretaker (a forwarder with a gate that revokes it) and the membrane (a forwarder wrapping everything that passes through it) are patterns of that literature, in E and in JavaScript (Miller; Van Cutsem and Miller on membranes); `CaptureFree` plays the role of the `DeepFrozen` auditor of E, the guarantee that a value is closed.
Capability safety of languages, and reasoning about what code can do from the capabilities it holds: Maffeis, Mitchell and Taly (Object capabilities and isolation of untrusted web applications, 2010) formalize capability safety for JavaScript subsets; Drossopoulou, Noble and Miller (The need for capability policies, 2013; Swapsies on the Internet, 2015) and Drossopoulou, Noble, Mackay and Eisenbach (holistic specifications) specify what an object capability guarantees against untrusted code; Devriese, Birkedal and Piessens (Reasoning about object capabilities with logical relations and effect parametricity, 2016) prove authority bounds from types; Melicher, Shi, Potanin and Aldrich (A capability-based module system for authority control, 2017) make modules of Wyvern receive their authority as capabilities. Gordon et al. (Uniqueness and reference immutability for safe parallelism, 2012) is the reference capability side that Fearless builds on: `iso`, `mut`, `read`, `imm` are reference capabilities, and `System` is an object capability; the attenuation design is where the two meet, since reconstruction rests on `CaptureFree` and Observable Determinism, which are typing facts.
Erlang spawns by module, function and arguments rather than by closure for the same reason reconstruction works here: a name travels, a closure does not. Fearless adds that the function named is pure by typing, so the spawned process is known to reach the same authority its parent describes.
What is new: in the object capability literature authority crosses a boundary by transferring a reference; here nothing is transferred, the new process reconstructs its authority from names, and the reconstruction is correct because the type system makes the construction a deterministic computation. Observable Determinism turns a description (a list of type names) into a faithful account of an object graph (the attenuated `System`): reconstruction is as good as transfer, and cheaper, since a name is portable and an object is not.
The claim to make: attenuation composed by reconstruction gives capability attenuation that is at the same time dynamic (chosen per run, per main, by the user or by the calling main) and statically known (the names are the whole policy), across operating system processes, with no serialization of capabilities.
